WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.340
Film Courage: How did you spend your first
few years as a screenwriter?

00:00:03.340 --> 00:00:05.819
I know you said you initially wanted to be
an actor?

00:00:05.819 --> 00:00:10.740
And I know you’ve had a career as a musician
but how did you spend your first few years

00:00:10.740 --> 00:00:12.840
as an actual screenwriter?

00:00:12.840 --> 00:00:14.259
How is it for you now?

00:00:14.259 --> 00:00:18.870
Is it the same (just more year behind you
in terms of what you don’t like, what you

00:00:18.870 --> 00:00:19.970
do like?

00:00:19.970 --> 00:00:27.769
Shawn Christensen, co-screenwriter/producer
of THE VANISHING OF SIDNEY HALL movie: It’s

00:00:27.769 --> 00:00:35.340
much different….so when I first wrote screenplays,
you are writing it for the fun of the medium

00:00:35.340 --> 00:00:38.960
or you want to write movies or be in the film
business or you have a great story to tell,

00:00:38.960 --> 00:00:43.570
whatever your reasons are (a combination of
all those things).

00:00:43.570 --> 00:00:50.870
But what happened for me is we had an agent
as I said from this little script and once

00:00:50.870 --> 00:00:55.100
you have an agent especially if you are at
a big agency like WME [William Morris Endeavor]

00:00:55.100 --> 00:01:02.760
you have access and when you have access it
charges you to…now you know you can really

00:01:02.760 --> 00:01:06.010
write, because you know you can get the screenplay
to people.

00:01:06.010 --> 00:01:08.620
It’s a different thing.

00:01:08.620 --> 00:01:17.800
And so I wrote a couple bigger movies, bigger
in…action movies I guess we’ll call them.

00:01:17.800 --> 00:01:25.620
And these movies went into the big studios
and they went through the engine (The Factory).

00:01:25.620 --> 00:01:31.540
And the writer can get really destroyed in
that Factory.

00:01:31.540 --> 00:01:33.790
And I got destroyed in that Factory.

00:01:33.790 --> 00:01:42.320
It doesn’t happen all the time but I wrote
a couple popcorn movies and they got rewritten

00:01:42.320 --> 00:01:43.320
head-to-toe.

00:01:43.320 --> 00:01:47.210
One of them didn’t get made, one did.

00:01:47.210 --> 00:01:54.090
But it was an excruciating experience and
it really quite frankly made me not want to

00:01:54.090 --> 00:01:55.980
write anymore.

00:01:55.980 --> 00:02:00.210
That’s really when I got into directing
because my feeling was I don’t know if I’m

00:02:00.210 --> 00:02:05.240
a good director but I really don’t know
if I’m a good writer (was the thing)?

00:02:05.240 --> 00:02:13.180
So maybe if I shoot my words myself like a
short film.

00:02:13.180 --> 00:02:19.420
I’ll know if I really belong here or not,
you know?

00:02:19.420 --> 00:02:25.700
And I did this short film called BRINK which
didn’t have a lot of dialogue in it.

00:02:25.700 --> 00:02:34.110
It was a very short film, kind of a sci-fi
romance, real quiet film, beautiful music

00:02:34.110 --> 00:02:35.900
by my composer Darren Morze.

00:02:35.900 --> 00:02:39.340
I actually made the film based on his music.

00:02:39.340 --> 00:02:42.690
And it was refreshing because as I said it
didn’t have a lot of dialogue so I really

00:02:42.690 --> 00:02:45.740
didn’t have a script, I didn’t write a
script for this film.

00:02:45.740 --> 00:02:52.230
And it was fun to just direct something, not
write it because I really didn’t think I

00:02:52.230 --> 00:02:55.360
was a good writer at that point.

00:02:55.360 --> 00:02:59.280
And it was nice to just work with friends.

00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:05.190
I had a camera and we shot this short film
and we started just doing short films and

00:03:05.190 --> 00:03:11.850
myself and my producing partner Damon Russell
and then I got into directing.

00:03:11.850 --> 00:03:21.170
And that kind of rounded me back to liking
to write again because I realized that I can

00:03:21.170 --> 00:03:24.709
have confidence in some of what I do.

00:03:24.709 --> 00:03:29.489
I don’t have confidence in most of what
I do but I can have confidence in writing

00:03:29.489 --> 00:03:31.900
again once I started directing.

